asgard-gnome-desktop — operator-facing metapackage for the GNOME group
======================================================================

What this is
------------

A metapackage: a .deb with no files, just a `Depends:` list naming the
packages that make up Asgard's GNOME desktop.  Installing it pulls
the whole group via apt's dependency resolver.  Removing it (with
`--autoremove`) cleans the whole group back out.

Why
---

The same package set is already curated in two places:

  config/pkg.list                                 [gnome-desktop]
  fork/source/athena-tasksel/tasks/gnome-desktop  Key: list

This metapackage adds a third surface aimed at the operator on a
booted Asgard system: a single name they can apt-install, apt-pin,
apt-remove, or query via `apt-cache rdepends`.

Pattern B (per docs/branding-methodology.md) — it does NOT replace
either pkg.list or the tasksel Key list.  It coexists.

How to build + test
-------------------

1. cache build / cache parse (fork/ scan + cache index pickup)
2. source build asgard-gnome-desktop
3. ls repo/dists/<codename>/main/binary-amd64/asgard-gnome-desktop_1.0.0_all.deb

To test on a running system:

  dpkg -i asgard-gnome-desktop_1.0.0_all.deb     # likely fails — no deps
  apt -f install                                   # resolves the Depends

Or via the cdrom pool after `iso build installer` ships it:

  apt install asgard-gnome-desktop

Or test the package shape without installing:

  dpkg-deb --info asgard-gnome-desktop_1.0.0_all.deb     # control + Depends
  dpkg-deb --contents asgard-gnome-desktop_1.0.0_all.deb # near-empty

How to ship (separately, after testing)
---------------------------------------

This source tree only adds the source pkg to fork/.  To make it
INSTALLABLE by default on Asgard installs, you'd also:

  1. config/pool.list — add `asgard-gnome-desktop` so it ships in /cdrom/pool
  2. (optional) config/pkg.list [gnome-desktop] — replace the long Key list
     with just `asgard-gnome-desktop`, letting apt's resolver expand it
  3. (optional) fork/source/athena-tasksel/tasks/gnome-desktop — same
     collapse on the tasksel side
  4. test_pkg_list_groups_mirror_tasksel_keys — extend to check the
     metapackage Depends == the pkg.list seeds

Steps 2-4 are Pattern A territory — decide separately after testing
the Pattern B build round-trip.

Source-tree contents
--------------------

  debian/changelog                          version + maintainer + date
  debian/control                            Source + Package + Depends:
  debian/copyright                          Apache-2.0 boilerplate
  debian/rules                              one-liner: `%: dh $@`
  debian/source/format                      3.0 (native) — no tarball
  debian/asgard-gnome-desktop.install       paths-to-ship list
  debian/asgard-gnome-desktop.postinst      reload NetworkManager hook
  conf/10-asgard-managed.conf               NM conf.d drop-in
  README                                    this file

Why the NetworkManager conf.d drop-in
-------------------------------------

A debootstrap-based installer leaves /etc/network/interfaces with a
default `eth0` (or similar) stanza configuring DHCP for the install-
time interface.  Stock NetworkManager then refuses to manage that
device — defers to ifupdown — and GNOME Shell sees zero NM-managed
devices, so the top-bar network indicator never appears.

Our drop-in flips the [ifupdown] managed default to true.  Ships at
/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/10-asgard-managed.conf — a higher-priority
override than NetworkManager.conf, doesn't conflict with the file
network-manager itself ships.

The postinst runs `systemctl try-reload-or-restart NetworkManager`
so the drop-in takes effect immediately on `apt install` without
requiring a reboot.  Idempotent: silent failure when NM isn't
running yet (e.g. during chroot bring-up) is fine because NM
re-reads its config on the next start.

dpkg treats the conf.d file as a conffile (it lives under /etc/),
so operator edits to it survive package upgrades.  apt remove
--autoremove asgard-gnome-desktop takes the drop-in with it.
